we're joined now by former senior  adviser to president  obama valerie jarrett she's attended two  presidential inaugurations valerie good  morning thanks for being with us  thank you good morning to you all this  is certainly going to be a very  different inauguration given  covet and the conser security  precautions do you have any concerns  about safety at the capitol for the  inauguration  i had the opportunity anthony to work  with the secret service  for all eight years of president obama  and vice president biden's term  and there are no more professional  competent men and women that i know so  i'm confident that they'll do everything  possible to keep  keep people safe conspicuously  conspicuously absent at this  inauguration  will be the outgoing president it's the  first time an outgoing president has  skipped an inauguration in 152 years  what sort of message do you think that  sends to the world  well anthony uh there's been nothing  conventional about this president since  day one and so i don't think it should  come as any surprise  to anyone that he would also not  continue with the tradition  of showing this smooth transition of  power as you know he didn't cooperate  early on with the transition he has yet  to acknowledge uh that joe biden will be  the next president  and so it's really no surprise and i  think what's refreshing though  is that in one day we will have new  leadership in america  and that's the reason for the rest of  the world particularly our allies around  the world  to be um overjoyed valerie how important  though do you think are those traditions  i remember very vividly when michelle  and barack obama stood outside the white  house and welcomed the trumps and listen  there doesn't seem to be any love lost  between those two families at the time  because donald trump had said some very  nasty things about  barack obama michelle obama wrote in her  book about how she felt that you know  her family was in danger because of  because of donald trump but yet they  welcomed them welcomed them very  graciously to the white house at that  time  how important are these traditions for  americans to see that we  are that it it can be peaceful and  should be peaceful  yeah it's a good question gail i was  actually there that day and it was  excruciatingly painful i remember as the  obama said goodbye to the house staff  people who had  taken such good care of them over the  years many of them  older and black who had lived to see a  black president and  first lady in the relationships there  and i saw how  incredibly gracious president and mrs  obama  were to the trumps and you're right they  weren't at all happy about the occasion  but they rose to the occasion  and that's what you're supposed to do we  lead by example here in this country  and so those traditions are very  important they symbolize the strength of  our democracy  but we will move on and i think  beginning  midday tomorrow you'll see a whole new  tone from our new president and vice  president  where do you stand on the question of to  impeach or not to impeach you know there  are some people that are saying  goodbye good riddance let the healing  begin let's all move on and others say  no no no not so fast there should be  people should be held accountable and by  people i mean donald trump  and in order to have healing you have to  have accountability  at the same time the biden  administration is coming on with a whole  bunch of ugly on their plate  where do you stand on this question well  what's  uh good about the biden administration  is its ability to multitask and i think  what  uh president-elect biden has said is  he's going to focus on the american  people  tackling the covid rebuilding our  economy reversing many of the  deletorious actions that president trump  took including the muslim ban  giving certainty to the dreamers  rejoining the climate  accord rejoining the world health  organization putting science back in  our efforts and so he will move forward  on that effort and then i think it's up  to the senate to decide  uh when they move forward with this  trial and i think it's not just about  holding president trump accountable  uh for inciting a riot in an attempt to  overthrow  our government and the smooth transition  of power but it's also a message to  everyone who follows in his footsteps  about what is acceptable behavior and  what is not  at the same time valerie can be it can  be a very disruptive and very divisive  event  at the beginning of an administration  which is trying to get off on a strong  foot and deal with  with a an escalating covet crisis so  there's a delicate balance there now  right  well of course there is a delicate  balance and i think it's going to be up  to the leadership in the senate to make  sure for example  anthony that president biden's cabinet  has  confirmed that needs to happen swiftly  given the magnitude of the challenges  that we're facing  and at the same time as they're  conducting a trial they will have to do  two things at once and  it is a very challenging time but the  house has already moved forward with its  impeachment and so it will be up to the  senate to have a trial  and i they would hopefully do it as  expeditiously but as thoroughly and as  transparently as possible  because the american people will be  watching the american people the world  is going to be watching as you know and  history is going to be made the world la  harris and  chief justice uh sonia sotomayera who's  going to be giving her the oath  judge uh justice sotomayor is going to  be holding the bible when kamala harris  takes the oath  that's going to be quite a sight to see  yes it will gail  i have goosebumps just when you mention  it the first woman the first person of  color  as a vice president oh my gosh tune in  everybody  yes thank you valerie jarrett we will  see you tomorrow
 

